{"input_text": "Question: If $P(A_1\cap A_2\cap...\cap A_{n-1}) > 0$ so : 
 $$P(A_1\cap A_2\cap A_3\cap A_4) = P(A_1)\cdot P(A_2|A_1)\cdot P(A_3|A_2\cap A_1)\cdot P(A_4|A_3\cap A_2\cap A_1)$$  
 I don't understand how to calculate $P(A_1\cap A_2\cap...A_{n-1})$ if I don't know whether they're independent or not. 

Answer: Note that $P(A\mid B)$ is defined by $\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}$ which yields $P(A\cap B)=P(A\mid B)P(B)$. So \begin{align}
P(A_1\cap A_2 \cap A_3 \cap A_4)&=P(A_4\cap (A_3 \cap A_2 \cap A_1))\\&=P(A_4\mid A_3 \cap A_2 \cap A_1)P(A_3 \cap (A_2 \cap A_1))\\&=P(A_4\mid A_3 \cap A_2 \cap A_1)P(A_3 \mid A_2 \cap A_1)P(A_2\cap A_1)\\&=P(A_4\mid A_3 \cap A_2 \cap A_1)P(A_3 \mid A_2 \cap A_1)P(A_2\mid A_1)P(A_1).
\end{align} 
